######## A. The agent walks api to done with next

(hook) start
kagebunshin: this folder is in project api, registered with kagebunshin. Its secrets come from kagebunshin, not from a .env file: run commands that need them as `kagebunshin run -- COMMAND` (`--env ENV` for another environment). Writing a .env file here is refused. The kagebunshin skill has the rest. It has not been shown to work yet: run `kagebunshin next` and do what it says.

$ kagebunshin next
project api, environment dev: 3 of 5 keys set
next (you): set LOG_LEVEL as a plain setting to .env.example's value, only if that is the real setting and not a placeholder (a secret belongs in the store or a provider)
  kagebunshin project set-var api dev LOG_LEVEL info

$ kagebunshin project set-var api dev LOG_LEVEL info
set LOG_LEVEL for environment dev of project api

$ kagebunshin next
project api, environment dev: 4 of 5 keys set
next (you): SIGNING_KEY is set by no source: `kagebunshin list --keys --prefix acme/dev/` names each secret under acme/dev/ and its keys (drop `--prefix` to look everywhere). Add the one that holds it with `kagebunshin project add-source api dev --provider aws_sm --secret NAME --profile acme-dev`; for a secret that is one plain string, not JSON, add `--key KEY` too. If none holds it, run `kagebunshin project add-source api dev --provider file`: the human then types its value into the local store
  kagebunshin list --keys --prefix acme/dev/

$ kagebunshin list --keys --prefix acme/dev/
4 matches
  acme/dev/api      aws_sm  API_KEY, API_URL
  acme/dev/db       aws_sm  DATABASE_URL
  acme/dev/queue    aws_sm  QUEUE_URL
  acme/dev/signing  aws_sm  (a plain string, not JSON)

$ kagebunshin project add-source api dev --provider aws_sm --secret acme/dev/signing --key SIGNING_KEY --profile acme-dev
added a source to environment dev of project api

$ kagebunshin next
project api, environment dev: 5 of 5 keys set, not yet proven
next (you): prove the setup: run the project's tests or its start command with its keys; pick the command
  kagebunshin run --strict -- COMMAND

-- a failing command proves nothing

$ kagebunshin run --strict -- false
[exit 1]

$ kagebunshin next
project api, environment dev: 5 of 5 keys set, not yet proven
next (you): prove the setup: run the project's tests or its start command with its keys; pick the command
  kagebunshin run --strict -- COMMAND

$ kagebunshin run --strict -- ./test.sh
ok   API_KEY
ok   API_URL
ok   DATABASE_URL
ok   SIGNING_KEY
ok   LOG_LEVEL

$ kagebunshin next
project api, environment dev: 5 of 5 keys set, proven; an environment named production is left to mark
next (you): mark api.prod sensitive: it is production by its name, so its values are handed out only once the human approves the request
  kagebunshin mark env api prod

-- 78: the prod environment the draft took from deploy-prod.yml is the agent's to mark

$ kagebunshin mark env api prod
marked sensitive

$ kagebunshin next
project api, environment dev: done. 5 of 5 keys set; `./test.sh` ran with them on <day>. They come from aws_sm:acme/dev/api (2), aws_sm:acme/dev/db (1), aws_sm:acme/dev/signing (1), the project file (1).

$ kagebunshin --json next
{"project":"api","env":"dev","done":true,"keys":{"set":5,"wanted":5},"headline":"project api, environment dev: done. 5 of 5 keys set; `./test.sh` ran with them on <day>. They come from aws_sm:acme/dev/api (2), aws_sm:acme/dev/db (1), aws_sm:acme/dev/signing (1), the project file (1).","step":null,"human":[]}

$ kagebunshin status
daemon: unlocked
providers:
  aws_sm  profile acme-dev  ok
project: api
env: dev
5 keys
  API_KEY       aws_sm:acme/dev/api
  API_URL       aws_sm:acme/dev/api
  DATABASE_URL  aws_sm:acme/dev/db
  LOG_LEVEL     project file             = info
  SIGNING_KEY   aws_sm:acme/dev/signing
shared with other environments (marking dev sensitive leaves these unmarked unless noted; a human can mark them as well in the app):
  aws_sm:acme/dev/api  also read by mono-worker.dev
every key set; `./test.sh` ran with them on <day>
runs in the last day: 2, last at <time>

